221 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 104 0 R
/T (2. INTRODUCTION )
/E (2. INTRODUCTION )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
222 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 104 0 R
/E (Software today is released more frequently than ever, and manual testing cannot keep up with this speed. A bug that reaches production can cause financial loss, security problems, and loss of user trust. DevOps addresses this by joining development and operations teams and automating the build, test, and deployment stages. Within this approach, unit testing is the first and fastest quality gate: each function is tested on its own, using known inputs and expected outputs, to confirm that it behaves correctly. This project, Unit Testing using DevOps, builds a simple application and attaches an automated test suite to it. A Git repository stores the source code, and a CI pipeline runs the tests on every push or pull request. If any test fails, the pipeline stops and the developer is notified, so faulty code is never deployed. The project therefore shows the complete path from writing code and tests to automatic verification and containerised delivery. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
