227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 105 0 R
/E (Software testing has long been recognised as a critical part of the development life cycle. Traditional approaches placed testing at the end of the project, after development was complete. Studies of such models show that defects found late are far more expensive to fix than defects found early. This observation led to test-driven development and the 'shift-left' idea, in which testing starts alongside coding. Unit testing frameworks such as JUnit, NUnit, unittest, and pytest give developers a standard way to write repeatable tests that check the behaviour of individual functions. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 105 0 R
/E (The DevOps movement extended this idea by introducing Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery \(CI/CD\). In CI, developers merge their changes into a shared repository frequently, and an automated server builds the code and runs the test suite on every change. Tools such as Jenkins, GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and Azure DevOps are widely used for this purpose. Existing research and industry reports show that teams using automated CI pipelines deploy more often, recover from failures faster, and have fewer defects reaching production. Containers, such as those built with Docker, further help by providing the same environment for testing on every machine. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
229 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 105 0 R
/E (Based on this background, the proposed project combines a unit testing framework, a version control system, a CI pipeline, and Docker into one simple workflow. Rather than relying on manual test execution, every commit is validated automatically, and the results are reported immediately. This makes the approach suitable for students and small teams who want a clear, practical example of how unit testing fits into a DevOps pipeline. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
